{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-11311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y$ and the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-11311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{1}{2}y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/assets/29cf73562152cb8201c8e93545ca7f8416aa8412217cfc9dcdaf87e5b794d971.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=2.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3}-0)-(\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{2}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-11311" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59fb94a788f53441d81416cbfaf34fc8b55d68af210510bf04f352110862cd72"} {"question" "q-11311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-11311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{13}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-11311" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-78887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x+1}{4}},$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-1$ and $y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-78887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x+1}{4}} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =\\frac{x+1}{4} \\\\ 4{y}^{3} & =x+1 \\\\ x & =4{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4{y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}-y{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $|-2|=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-78887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59fb94a788f53441d81416cbfaf34fc8b55d68af210510bf04f352110862cd72"} {"question" "q-78887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-78887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-78887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-117482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=16-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-117482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{4} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/assets/488788313bd32999611a520ec26e4ba6fd68a56e0333a7252a0faaf7107cad90.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=16-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=-2$ to $y=2.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(16-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =16\\cdot y{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\cdot (2-(-2))-(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =64-\\frac{64}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-117482" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59fb94a788f53441d81416cbfaf34fc8b55d68af210510bf04f352110862cd72"} {"question" "q-117482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-117482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "51"] ["math" "\\frac{128}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{256}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{512}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{64}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-117482" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{256}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-346674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area $A$ of the closed region between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\le 0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-346674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y({y}^{4}-16) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y({y}^{2}-4)({y}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y(y-2)(y+2)({y}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/assets/03737836a352eafaf486e7e5f71d882ac39715e788a0c9113eb809b185584bdc.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-2$ to $y=0.$\nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(0-(-64))-\\frac{3}{2}(0-4) \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-346674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59fb94a788f53441d81416cbfaf34fc8b55d68af210510bf04f352110862cd72"} {"question" "q-346674" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-346674" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-346735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x+\\frac{1}{4}}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-2$ and $y=-1.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-346735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =x+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,|}_{-2}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-4)-\\frac{1}{4}[-1-(-2)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-4|=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-346735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59fb94a788f53441d81416cbfaf34fc8b55d68af210510bf04f352110862cd72"} {"question" "q-346735" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"} {"question" "q-346735" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032052/state.json" "file_sha256" "56cef9f5524039a0bb50c9eec1acce3c60f8650b7c7df07a691ae1cc85f2579b"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1218 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
